\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSObject protocol groups methods that are fundamental to all Objective-C objects. If an object conforms to this protocol, it can be considered a first-class object in NEXTSTEP. Such an object can be asked about its: \
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab Class, and the place of its class in the inheritance hierarchy \
\'b7\tab Conformance to protocols\
\'b7\tab Ability to respond to a particular message\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 In addition, objects that conform to this protocol\'d0with its retain, release, and autorelease methods\'d0can also integrate with the object-management and deallocation scheme defined in the Foundation Kit. (See the introduction to the Foundation Kit for more information.) Thus, an object that conforms to the NSObject protocol can be managed by container objects like those defined by NSArray and NSDictionary.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NEXTSTEP\'27s root class, NSObject, adopts this protocol, so virtually all objects in NEXTSTEP have the features described by this protocol.\
\fs14\
