\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(id)\b performSelector:\b0 (SEL)\i aSelector\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Sends an \i aSelector\i0  message to the receiver and returns the result of the message.  If \i aSelector\i0  is NULL, an NSInvalidArgumentException is raised.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b performSelector:\b0  is equivalent to sending an \i aSelector\i0  message directly to the receiver.  For example, all three of the following messages do the same thing:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 id myClone = [anObject copy];\
\fi0 id myClone = [anObject performSelector:@selector(copy)];\
\fi0 id myClone = [anObject performSelector:sel_getUid("copy")];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 However, the \b performSelector:\b0  method allows you to send messages that aren\'27t determined until run time.  A variable selector can be passed as the argument:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 SEL myMethod = findTheAppropriateSelectorForTheCurrentSituation();\
[anObject performSelector:myMethod];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \i aSelector\i0  should identify a method that takes no arguments.  For methods that return anything other than an object, use NSInvocation.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b performSelector:withObject:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b performSelector:withObject:withObject:\
\fs14\
\fs46\
